# Former Gods, Part 1
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 1

The gods on whose behalf people dared destroy thinkers are gone. Deities once magnificently worshipped by millions in Babylon, Greece, Rome, Egypt, China, and elsewhere now have neither worship, temples, nor drums. Greece today honours Socrates, but the gods he was accused of insulting and for whom he was poisoned no longer exist there.

## Former Gods

“Should lust overflow in God’s ceremonial hall? People, do you know what became of Zeus and his lawful wife Hera? You say only one who subdues lust and anger receives divine grace, and that worship leads to purity and liberation. Then hear calmly what a devotee admitted into heaven did in God’s own presence.

“Ixion, king of the Lapiths, reached heaven as a supposedly virtuous man. Instead of worshipping Zeus, he desired Hera and approached the Mother of the world. Zeus punished him. How did one worthy of heaven retain such lust, and how did he receive grace?

“Can such obscene tales describe transcendent God? Consider Zeus himself. He drove out his father, seized the throne, shared power with brothers who aided his plot, and married his sister. Your own stories make him power-hungry, deceitful, and absorbed in pleasure. Can this be divine character? What morality can such a god cultivate? Would the world respect worshippers of grotesque multi-headed, many-armed, human-sacrifice-demanding figures? Is this religion or reason, and should national wealth be wasted upon it?”

Thus Diagoras thundered in fifth-century BCE Greece against obscenities committed in God’s name and stories fabricated by knaves. His teaching aroused anger rather than enlightenment. People called him blasphemer and demanded his destruction. He fled his native Melos and died in Corinth.

Though he ran for his life, Diagoras ultimately won through his successors. Rational thought reappeared in Socrates, who likewise condemned superstition and temple obscenity. Diagoras was driven away; Socrates did not flee or submit but was killed in Greece. Between them reason had grown from speech alone to a conviction for which people would suffer and die.

Those who mocked fleeing Diagoras fell silent when Socrates calmly drank poison for principle. His death gave reason new force and prestige. At his trial, 281 of 501 judges voted guilty and 220 not guilty: antiquity’s army exceeded reason’s by only sixty-one. A substantial part of the town now defended inquiry.

Socrates was charged with calling Greek gods false and corrupting youth. He said many good people had already suffered from popular ignorance and many more would follow. His blood watered the conviction that fear of death would not halt rational work. As this “ablution” of knowledge continued, ritual ablutions for ancient gods diminished.

Five centuries after Socrates, Jesus compelled old priesthoods to summon all their gods to battle, not by killing but by being crucified. Jesus was nailed to the cross, yet the old gods died. Countless lesser deities in splendid temples were forgotten, sacrifices stopped, and hereditary priests lost employment.

People destroyed thinkers while defending gods, art, poetry, and temples, but to what effect? Those gods are gone. The poison Socrates drank killed many deities. Their former worshippers now admit such tales belonged to an age of ignorance.

There are too many former gods to describe fully, some without even surviving names. Before rational martyrs arose, Greece, Rome, Norway, Sweden, China, Egypt, and other lands possessed divine crowds, epics, exploits, and sacred sports like ours today. The difference is that their theology clarified centuries ago while our ancient outlook remains.

Making those gods former did not destroy their peoples, art, poetry, sculpture, painting, or faith. Ignorance receded and a purer doctrine of one formless God arose. They too had counterparts to Mari, Manmatha, Katteri, Sani, Vayu, and Varuna, with erotic mythology. They discarded it and flourished; we remain poor and subjugated.

Here are a few former gods once praised as protectors of kingdoms.

### Apollo

Apollo was a major Greek god, worshipped like our sun-god.

### Abaddon

Guardian of hell, he inspected visitors for God’s seal and cast the unmarked into the abyss for torment.

### Aeolus

The Greek wind-god separated good and bad winds into leather bags and released their effects.

### Aphrodite

Greek goddess of beauty and mother of Cupid, whose arrows caused love. Ares desired her and was exposed and mocked by the gods.

### Ares

The Greek war-god delighted in battle and was humiliated after his affair with Aphrodite was discovered.

### Artemis

A major virgin goddess like Apollo, Artemis protected animals.

### Bacchus

Son of Zeus and god of intoxicating drink, Bacchus rode a chariot drawn by tigers.

### Balder

Norway’s handsome and wise sun-god, son of Odin and Frigg, was killed by mischievous Loki.

### Bellona

Roman war-goddess, variously called wife, sister, or daughter of Mars, who travelled bearing a torch and attended to war.

### Bootes

Greek inventor of the plough and god of agriculture.

### Boreas

God of the north wind.

### Bragi

Norwegian god of poetry, son of Odin and Frigg, portrayed as a white-bearded elder welcoming those killed in battle into heaven.

### Clytie

A divine woman who loved Apollo; abandoned by him, she wasted away and became a sunflower.

### Lugh

Irish sun-god.

### Cupid

Rome’s god of love, portrayed as a blindfolded child with bow and arrows.

### Cybele

In northern Asia, Cybele was worshipped as goddess of natural beauty through lavish, drunken dancing.

### Diana

Roman goddess of moonlight, an eternally virgin huntress. Her temple at Ephesus was counted among the wonders of the world.

### Dionysus

Greek counterpart of Bacchus, worshipped through intoxicated revelry. His mother died pregnant; Zeus removed and nurtured him in his thigh.

### The Three Sisters

The Greek Fates were Clotho, Lachesis, and Atropos. One spun, another measured or wove, and the third cut the thread of human life.

### Pan

Descendant of a Greek satyr-like deity, Pan was worshipped in Latium as guardian of fields and livestock.

### Fides

Romans deified fidelity to one’s word as Fides, building her a temple and festival.

### Flora

Roman goddess of gardens and flowers.

### Freyr

Norse god associated with forests, light, rain, peace, and prosperity.

### Frigg

Norse earth-goddess and wife of Odin.

### Gad

Babylonian deity of fortune.

### Demeter

Greek mother of all living beings on earth and above.

### Ganymede

Zeus desired the handsome mortal Ganymede, made him divine, and kept him nearby to pour drink.

### Hathor

Egyptian queen of heaven.

### Hebe

Beautiful daughter of Zeus, worshipped as a goddess and married in heaven to the dead Greek hero Hercules.

### Hecate

Three-headed Greek goddess ruling earth, heaven, and hell with countless spirits as servants.

### Heimdall

Norse guardian of heaven’s rainbow bridge, able to hear grass grow. He and destructive Loki fought repeatedly and ultimately killed each other.

### Hel

Norse goddess of death and daughter of Loki.

### Hera

Zeus married his sister Hera. Jealous of his love for mortals, she persecuted his children.

### Hermes

Like our Narada, Hermes, son of Zeus and Maia, was divine messenger and trickster, wearing winged cap and sandals.

### Hesperides

When Hera married Zeus, the divine maidens appointed to guard the golden apples given as dowry...

---NOTES---
Part 1 begins as a self-contained opening of the “Former Gods” serial. It ends mid-entry after the Hesperides’ duty; `maji_kadavulgal2.md` completes the sentence by naming and noting their worship.

The source spells the thinker’s name `டையகோராஸ்`, identifiable as Diagoras of Melos, and its place `காரிநித்`, rendered as Corinth. The trial arithmetic in the source says 501 judges, 281 guilty, 220 not guilty, but then states a difference of 67; the actual difference is 61. The Tamil source remains unchanged and the English uses the internally derived figure.

Many parenthetical Latin names are severely damaged by OCR. Conventional English identifications are used where the Tamil and description support them: Apollo, Abaddon, Aeolus, Aphrodite, Ares, Artemis, Bacchus, Balder, Bellona, Bootes, Boreas, Bragi, Clytie, Lugh, Cupid, Cybele, Diana, Dionysus, Clotho, Lachesis, Atropos, Pan, Fides, Flora, Freyr, Frigg, Gad, Demeter, Ganymede, Hathor, Hebe, Hecate, Heimdall, Hel, Hera, Hermes, and Hesperides. Tamil OCR is preserved verbatim.

The source’s Pan genealogy and some geographical labels are compressed or nonstandard. The translation stays close to the recoverable description rather than silently substituting a modern mythological reference entry.
